`1.0 == 1`, same hash), and `str()` returns the original string.\n- `Constraint.parse` handles bare names, comma-separated clauses, and all six operators. `allows()` takes a `Version` or a string.\n- `resolve` runs a depth-first search. It always expands the alphabetically smallest pending package and tries its versions highest first. Each choice is forward-checked against the constraints already accumulated, so impossible branches are pruned early. Cycles work because already-assigned packages are only checked, not re-expanded.\n- On failure it raises `ResolutionError` naming the package where the search dead-ended, falling back to the root only if nothing else was recorded.\n\n**Tests cover:** the spec example, numeric ordering, highest-version preference, alphabetical priority, a backtracking case, unreachable packages excluded, self and mutual cycles, unsatisfiable chains naming the leaf (`b`), a missing package, and a timing check on a 300-version index.\n\n**Caveats**\n- The timing test is weak. Its dependency structure is simple, so it does not really stress the search. The hidden test's index is unknown, so performance on it is unverified.\n- The error-naming rule is a heuristic: it reports the deepest failure seen during search. That matches the spec's example, but other failure shapes could name a different package than the hidden tests expect.\n- The tests are plain `unittest`, so they run without pytest.", "ttft_ms": 1245, "type": "result", "duration_ms": 47359, "uuid": "425ba34f-58f4-4123-b92e-a3048f471c7e", "ttft_stream_ms": 753, "time_to_request_ms": 161, "first_content_frame_ms": 753, "queued_turn_count": 0, "result_index": 0, "timestamp": "2026-10-09T17:08:59.787Z"}